/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 167 0 R
/E (single, momentary readings. In contrast, a CGM system uses a small sensor inserted just under the skin, usually on the abdomen or arm, to measure glucose level in the interstitial fluid. The sensor contains an enzyme \(glucose oxidase\) that reacts with glucose and generates electrical signals, which is converted into real-time readings.)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
680 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 167 0 R
/E (These readings are sent automatically to a smartphone or receiver every few minutes, allowing continuous tracking throughout the day and night. CGM not only shows current levels but also trends whether glucose is rising, falling, or stable-and provides alerts for highs and lows, helping prevent emergencies. It also introduces the concept of "Time in Range" giving a clearer picture of overall control. However, CGM comes at a cost. typically ranging from 3,000 to 6,000 per sensor in India, with each sensor lasting about 10 to 14 days, making it a recurring expense. Despite this, its ability to reduce pain, provide constant insights, and support better decision- making makes it a powerful tool in improving diabetes management and quality of life. )
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
